message: If you use this work, please cite it as below.
title: 'Beyond Philosophical Distance: When Pairwise Checks Decide Joint Compatibility'
authors:
- name: PIATRA Institute
date-released: '2026-11-01'
url: https://piatra.institute/papers/beyond-philosophical-distance.pdf
repository-code: https://github.com/piatra-institute/papers-beyond-philosophical-distance
abstract: Maps of philosophical positions are built from pairwise relations such as distances, correlations and shared citations. A synthesis of commitments can be compatible in every pair and incompatible as a whole. We ask when checking $k$ commitments at a time decides joint compatibility, and how much structure in position data lies beyond pairwise statistics. The first answer is the obstruction order of the commitment class, the size of its largest minimal incompatible family, which is its Helly number. Categorical answers and ranges on ordered scales have order two when every combination of answers is admissible. On $n$ yes/no issues, categorical answers together with implications have order $n+1$, as do parity constraints, and all two-literal clauses $2n$; convex regions have order $d+1$ in a continuous space, $2^d$ on a grid of ordinal positions, and at least six when evaluated on 30 occupied positions of a planar map. Exhaustive enumeration confirms the small cases. The empty cells of pairwise tables of observed answers exclude exactly the profiles outside the majority-closed hull of the observed profiles; their numerical values can exclude more, which linear programming finds and certifies. Two public datasets stand in for unreleased PhilPapers responses. In the judgments of 64,988 participants on three trolley cases, third-order structure carries 2.38% of the multi-information and is stable across countries and designs. Among 223 consciousness researchers none was detected, at a sample size at which screening all triplets missed an effect of the trolley size in all 100 simulated datasets.
